    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d481">

                <pb facs="0715" n="633" sameAs="frus:frus1925v02/pg633"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">817.1051/81: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Chargé in Nicaragua</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Thurston</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1925-06-16T14:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">June 16, 1925—2
                                p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>70. Major Carter<anchor n="10" xml:id="d481-f1"/> advises Department that he
                    signed one year contract with Nicaraguan Chargé d’Affaires on June 10th. He will
                    be “Chief of the Constabulary and of the School of Instruction of the National
                    Guard created by the legislative decree of May 14 of the present year” and will
                    prepare regulations for the school and for the constabulary in accord with the
                    Nicaraguan Government. He states that he expects to sail for Nicaragua in about
                    a week. The contract provides for the employment of four additional American <pb
                        facs="0716" n="634" xml:id="pg634"/> officers as assistants. Further
                    information is being sent to you by mail.</p>
                <p>While all arrangements have been made directly between Carter and the Nicaraguan
                    Legation without intervention by the Department, and while the Department feels
                    that it may contribute to the success of the new constabulary if it is regarded
                    as a purely Nicaraguan institution and the officials are regarded from the
                    outset as Nicaraguan employees having no connection with the United States
                    Government, it desires that you should extend every proper assistance to Major
                    Carter in the performance of his duties, and that you should keep the Department
                    very fully informed regarding the progress of his work.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Kellogg</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d481-f1" n="10" xml:id="d481-f1-content">
